A||N||5||-=shame=-||New to Fiero||||7||
Z||000000||-=shame=-||06-24-2000||09:36 AM||||Well that's right, I just bought my first ever Fiero. '86 GT V-6,  140,000. She's great! In just one week I've fallen in love. Of course with great joy comes great sorrow. So I need a little help. First of the mileage isn't good at all 16.5 mpg city. Part of this is caused by another problem, I replaced the rear left caliper. It started out as pads but the dust boot on the piston was torn, and just buying a new caliper for $33.00 was easier than fighting a rebuild kit. Besides, I had no idea how long it had been torn. Now on installation of the new caliper I couldn't locate "silicone grease" for the two T-50 Torx mounting bolts so I used a hi-temp grease. Bleeding them was more of a problem for my girlfriend to sit there and push on the brakes than you could imagine. ITS GRABBING!! I thought it might be the caliper, swapped it at the store, not it. PLUS it's only that side. If the line were crimped it wouldn't even close (or atleast I would think). Plaese help.||||reg||7
Z||000001||Flash Box||06-24-2000||12:29 PM||||Welcome to the board, I am kind of a new Fiero owner myself. I haven't had to mess with my brakes yet, but I am sure one of these guys & gals can give you the information you need. They have been real helpful to me so far.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||06-24-2000||01:49 PM||||Welcome  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!<P>My first guess would have to be the emergency brake.<P>Try loosening e-brake cable at the adjuster, located on cable under rear of car, aft of trunk box.  By the way, did the caliper slip over the brake rotor easily when you installed it?      <P>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!||||reg||6
Z||000003||-=shame=-||06-24-2000||05:39 PM||||Well here's an interesting part ... NO parking (or emergency) brake the previous owner (Tim The Tool Man Taylor) cut it. And yes ... it went on easy enough. No forcing or prying but still had to line it up. Now of course this is #2 of 2 on the calpiers. The first one, (I mounted the E brake spring) when i took it off the E brake would only ratchet it out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||06-24-2000||09:59 PM||||Well, now there's only one thing to do - is there some way you can blame it on your girlfriend   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> ??<P>Next thing I would try is this:<P>Loosen lug nuts on the offending wheel.  Raise and support car.  Try to rotate wheel.  If it's dragging, remove wheel.  See? Now the wheel rotates freely   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !!<P>Remove e-brake actuator lever (on inboard side of caliper) - 18mm socket.  Turn actuator lever (w/ 14mm socket) to retract the piston - in other words, opposite direction from the way pulling the e-brake cable would turn the lever.  Don't force it.<P>If the piston retracts this way, to where the caliper lets loose of the rotor and it turns freely, reinstall e-brake actuator lever, reinstall wheel, lower car and you're done.  I think -!<P>Otherwise (if the actuator doesn't want to turn), it means the piston doesn't want to retract.  Crack open the bleeder valve.  If fluid squirts out and you can retract piston now, it means you have an hydraulic lock.  <P>Might be the flex hose to the caliper is collapsed inside.  I have been told that brake hoses have been known to collapse on the inside, while the outside looked perfectly fine.  It wouldn't be a bad idea to replace the hose anyway, especially if you think it's been in there since 1986... <P>The story goes that the brakes would lock up after the brake pedal was nailed in a "panic stop" situation (for what it's worth, this was in a Camaro).  Apparently, the hose was acting like a check valve.<P>Which reminds me - ! There's a little rubber plug thingy in the end of the piston, that's supposed to be changed whenever you replace brake pads.  It's called a "2-way check valve" (the NAME doesn't even make sense to me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> ).  Haven't the slightest idea what it does, but I suppose it's worth trying, assuming you didn't change it already.     <P>Enough for now.  Be careful, be patient, and keep us posted   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !  <P>           <P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 06-24-2000).]||||reg||1
